{% comment %}
  sections/feature-cards.liquid

  A row of feature cards inside one bordered panel. Each card is a title, a
  ringed icon and a line of text; hovering one fills it with a picture and
  swaps the icon's colour.

  ## Nothing here is a third copy of anything

  The surface is `sections/featured-blog.liquid`'s, setting id for setting id —
  colour scheme, background media, the four border widths, the opacity slider,
  the two radii, the margins, the container. A merchant who has set up a
  Featured blog has set up this one, and the two share `snippets/spacing.liquid`,
  `snippets/background-media.liquid` and `snippets/container.liquid` rather than
  reimplementing them.

  The carousel is `sections/multicolumn.liquid`'s: the element is written out
  here because `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` cannot have its output captured, and
  `carousel-shell-attrs` / `carousel-shell-tail` supply the attributes and the
  controls from the same sub-snippets `carousel-shell.liquid` itself uses. Every
  `carousel_*` id is the one the other two sections already carry, so the
  arrows, the peek and the pagination are one implementation.

  ## Three frames, three sets of border settings

  The design has a border in two places and the theme has merchants who want it
  in a third, so the controls are grouped by which box they draw:

      Borders          the section frame — a dashed hairline across the top
      Cards panel      the dashed rounded box the cards sit inside
      (per card)       `blocks/_feature-card.liquid`, its own panel again

  They are three separate boxes, so they are three separate groups rather than
  one set of settings with a "what does this apply to" select. A select there
  would mean a merchant who wants both has to choose.

  All three ship drawn, and all three nest: a 40px section frame around a 40px
  panel around 24px cards. The widths are what separate them — the section is a
  single rule across the top, the panel is the full outline, the card is corners
  only — so the three do not read as one box drawn three times.

  ## Why the panel clips

  A card's hover picture fills the card edge to edge, and the outermost cards
  touch the panel's rounded corners. Without `overflow: hidden` on the panel a
  square photograph corner sits outside a rounded frame.

  So the panel clips — but only when it is actually rounded, because clipping
  unconditionally would cut the focus ring off the outermost card for no reason.
  `feature-cards__panel--clipped` is added when the radius is above zero.

  ## The controls are outside the panel, and that is why the id exists

  A clipping, bordered, rounded box is the wrong place for the arrows and the
  dots. Inside it they are within the frame the row is drawn in — pagination
  pressed against a dashed border, and arrows set to Outside cut away entirely
  by the corner radius. So the row is rendered under the panel and reaches the
  carousel by `data-carousel-for`, which is what `id:` on `carousel-shell-attrs`
  is for.

  `carousel-nav` and `carousel-controls` could already detach. `carousel-pagination`
  could not — `carousel.js` read `this.refs.pagination`, and `component.js`
  collects refs from inside the element only. It now resolves the pagination
  element the same way it already resolved the arrows: nested wins, and an
  external one is looked for only when the element has an id. Every other
  carousel in the theme renders its pagination nested and never reaches that
  lookup, so nothing else changes behaviour.

  There is no **Arrow position** setting. Detached, the row is under the panel
  and nowhere else, and a select offering Inside or Outside would be describing
  a track it no longer sits in.

  ## Default layout is a grid, and a phone is a slider

  Four features across is a row a desktop reads at a glance and a phone cannot
  finish. So `layout` defaults to `grid` and `carousel_on_mobile` to true, which
  is the one combination the pair of settings exists for. The same Type select
  as Featured blog, minus Editorial — that layout is a lead story and the cards
  around it, and a feature row has no lead feature.

  ## The cards arrive one at a time

  `<motion-effect>` wraps the panel with `data-target=".feature-card"` and a
  stagger, rather than each card carrying its own effect. A card is a merchant
  block here — `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` renders them all in one call and
  passes no index — so there is no `forloop.index0` to turn into a per-card
  delay the way `blocks/_article-card.liquid` does. One element that finds the
  cards by selector and staggers them is the same cascade with nothing to keep
  in step, and it survives a merchant reordering the row.

  Targeting rather than `data-children` is deliberate: the panel's children are
  the carousel element, not the cards, and once Swiper mounts it moves the
  cards into a wrapper of its own. A selector still finds them; a child list
  would not.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings

  assign desktop = s.columns_desktop | default: 4 | plus: 0

  comment
    Mobile columns is a select of two, clamped rather than trusted — a section
    saved at three before the control narrowed keeps the 3 in the settings data
    and the picker has nowhere to show it. Same guard, same reason, as
    `sections/featured-blog.liquid`.

    `plus: 0` coerces the select's string first: `"2" > 2` compares a string to
    an integer, which is false in every Liquid.
  endcomment
  assign cols_mobile = s.columns_mobile | default: 1 | plus: 0
  if cols_mobile > 2
    assign cols_mobile = 2
  endif
  if cols_mobile < 1
    assign cols_mobile = 1
  endif

  assign cols_tablet = s.columns_tablet | default: 2 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the surface, from `sections/featured-blog.liquid` ----
  endcomment
  assign background_media = s.background_media | default: 'none'
  assign has_background_media = false
  if background_media != 'none'
    assign has_background_media = true
  endif

  assign background_paint = ''
  if has_background_media == false
    assign background_paint = s.background_color | default: ''
  endif

  comment
    Opacity goes into the colour rather than onto the element: `opacity` on the
    section would fade the cards along with the frame.

    With no colour picked the border is `currentColor`, which the colour filters
    cannot take an alpha channel from — there is nothing to read until the
    scheme resolves in the browser. `color-mix` does the same fade at that point
    instead, so both paths honour the slider.
  endcomment
  assign border_line = s.border_style | default: 'dashed'

  assign bw_top = 0
  assign bw_right = 0
  assign bw_bottom = 0
  assign bw_left = 0
  assign border_any = 0

  if border_line != 'none'
    assign bw_top = s.border_width_top | default: 1 | plus: 0
    assign bw_right = s.border_width_right | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign bw_bottom = s.border_width_bottom | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign bw_left = s.border_width_left | default: 0 | plus: 0
    assign border_any = bw_top | plus: bw_right | plus: bw_bottom | plus: bw_left

    assign border_percent = s.border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.border_color == blank
      assign border_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: border_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign border_alpha = border_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign border_paint = s.border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', border_alpha
    endif
  endif

  comment
    ---- corners ----

    Four corners, twice — one set per device, behind the same Desktop/Mobile
    switch the padding and the gaps already use.

    The top pair ships at 40 and the bottom pair at 0, because a section is a
    band that lifts away from what is above it and sits flush on what is below:
    a rounded bottom edge leaves two slivers of the page showing through under
    it. They are four sliders rather than one number and a rule, so a merchant
    who wants a fully rounded card can still have one.

    They reach CSS as a single border-radius shorthand, in the order that
    property takes — top-left, top-right, bottom-right, bottom-left. Nothing in
    the stylesheet reads them as a bare number, which is what makes sending a
    shorthand safe.

    A phone gets its own four because the same number does not read the same at
    both widths: 40px is a soft lift across 1400px and a heavy scoop out of a
    390px screen. Each mobile corner falls back to its desktop value when the
    setting is missing entirely, so a section saved before these existed keeps
    one shape at every width.
  endcomment
  assign r_tl = s.border_radius_top_left | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign r_tr = s.border_radius_top_right | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign r_bl = s.border_radius_bottom_left | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign r_br = s.border_radius_bottom_right | default: 0 | plus: 0

  assign r_tl_m = s.border_radius_top_left_mobile | default: r_tl | plus: 0
  assign r_tr_m = s.border_radius_top_right_mobile | default: r_tr | plus: 0
  assign r_bl_m = s.border_radius_bottom_left_mobile | default: r_bl | plus: 0
  assign r_br_m = s.border_radius_bottom_right_mobile | default: r_br | plus: 0
  assign margin_top = s.margin_top | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign margin_bottom = s.margin_bottom | default: 0 | plus: 0

  comment
    ---- the cards panel ----

    The same three-part border resolution as the section frame, on its own
    prefix. One width rather than four: this box is the frame the design draws
    all the way round, and a merchant who wants a single rule under a row of
    features has the section's four-sided control one group up.
  endcomment
  assign panel_line = s.panel_border_style | default: 'dashed'
  assign panel_width = 0
  assign panel_paint = ''

  if panel_line != 'none'
    assign panel_width = s.panel_border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0

    assign panel_percent = s.panel_border_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    if s.panel_border_color == blank
      assign panel_paint = 'color-mix(in srgb, currentColor ' | append: panel_percent | append: '%, transparent)'
    else
      assign panel_alpha = panel_percent | divided_by: 100.0
      assign panel_paint = s.panel_border_color | color_modify: 'alpha', panel_alpha
    endif
  endif

  assign panel_radius = s.panel_border_radius | default: 40 | plus: 0
  assign panel_radius_mobile = s.panel_border_radius_mobile | default: panel_radius | plus: 0

  comment
    The two paddings are independent, and 0 on the phone means 0.

    This used to fall back to the desktop value whenever the mobile slider read
    0, because 0 was also the default and there was no way to tell "no inset"
    from "not answered yet". The phone's default is 6px now — the frame runs
    much closer to the edge of the screen there, and a card pressed against it
    reads as a mistake — so the default carries that meaning on its own and the
    fallback has nothing left to do except take 0 away from a merchant who
    deliberately dragged the slider to it.

    The section's own padding sliders keep the fallback: theirs still default to
    0, and the setting says so on its own label.
  endcomment
  assign panel_padding = s.panel_padding | default: 0 | plus: 0
  assign panel_padding_mobile = s.panel_padding_mobile | default: 6 | plus: 0

  assign panel_background = ''
  assign panel_tint = s.panel_background | default: '#FFFFFF'
  if panel_tint != blank
    assign panel_bg_alpha = s.panel_background_opacity | default: 100 | plus: 0
    assign panel_bg_alpha = panel_bg_alpha | divided_by: 100.0
    assign panel_background = panel_tint | color_modify: 'alpha', panel_bg_alpha
  endif

  comment
    A card's hover picture reaches the panel's corners, so the panel has to clip
    it — but only where there is a corner to clip to. An unconditional
    `overflow: hidden` would cut the focus ring off the outermost card, which is
    the same reasoning `.featured-blog.has-background-media` is written with.
  endcomment
  assign panel_clipped = false
  if panel_radius > 0 or panel_radius_mobile > 0
    assign panel_clipped = true
  endif

  comment
    ---- the carousel ----

    Two types, two answers. `grid` is a CSS grid; only `carousel` reaches
    `data-layout`, or the component would try to build a Swiper out of a layout
    that is not one.
  endcomment
  assign layout_type = s.layout | default: 'grid'

  assign cs_layout = 'grid'
  if layout_type == 'carousel'
    assign cs_layout = 'carousel'
  endif

  comment
    `false == blank` is true in Liquid, so a blank check would read "off" as
    "not set" and turn the mobile carousel back on. `nil` is the only value that
    means the merchant has not answered yet.
  endcomment
  assign carousel_on_mobile = s.carousel_on_mobile
  if carousel_on_mobile == nil
    assign carousel_on_mobile = true
  endif

  assign cs_layout_mobile = 'grid'
  if layout_type == 'carousel' or carousel_on_mobile
    assign cs_layout_mobile = 'carousel'
  endif

  assign cs_is_carousel = false
  if cs_layout == 'carousel' or cs_layout_mobile == 'carousel'
    assign cs_is_carousel = true
  endif

  comment
    The slide count, and only the slides.

    `carousel-options` uses it to decide whether the merchant's Loop setting is
    achievable — Swiper silently disables looping below twice `slidesPerView`,
    which reads as the setting doing nothing. So it has to be the number of
    cards, not `section.blocks.size`: an app block sitting in the row, or the
    static heading if the platform ever counts it, would inflate the number and
    let a loop through that Swiper then refuses.
  endcomment
  assign cs_count = 0
  for feature_block in section.blocks
    if feature_block.type == '_feature-card'
      assign cs_count = cs_count | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor

  assign cs_label = 'accessibility.carousel' | t

  comment
    ---- the controls, outside the panel ----

    The arrows and the pagination are rendered under the panel rather than
    inside it, and they reach the carousel by id.

    They have to be outside. The panel is a bordered, rounded, clipping box that
    the track sits inside, and controls within it are inside the frame the row
    is drawn in — dots pressed against a dashed border, and arrows set to
    Outside clipped away entirely by the corner radius.

    `carousel-nav` and `carousel-controls` could already do this: pass
    `carousel:` and they stamp `data-carousel-for`, which `findExternalControls`
    resolves from the document. `carousel-pagination` could not — the component
    read `this.refs.pagination`, and `component.js` collects refs from inside
    the element only, so a detached dot row was never found. Both now take the
    same parameter and `carousel.js` resolves the element the same way it
    already resolved the arrows: nested wins, external is looked for only when
    the element has an id. Every other carousel in the theme renders its
    pagination nested and never reaches that lookup.

    The id is the section's, so two of these sections on one page cannot claim
    each other's controls.
  endcomment
  assign carousel_id = 'feature-cards-' | append: section.id

  assign cs_pagination = s.carousel_pagination | default: 'dots'
  assign cs_pagination_align = s.carousel_pagination_align | default: 'center'

  assign cs_nav = s.carousel_navigation
  if cs_nav == nil
    assign cs_nav = false
  endif

  comment
    Whether the row is drawn at all is a Liquid question, not a runtime one.
    The layout is known here for both breakpoints, so the visibility is a class
    and a media query rather than a `:has()` test or a class the component has
    to remember to toggle — and a row of dots can never be left on a page where
    the cards are a static grid.
  endcomment
  assign controls_class = 'feature-cards__controls'
  if cs_layout == 'carousel'
    assign controls_class = controls_class | append: ' feature-cards__controls--always'
  elsif cs_layout_mobile == 'carousel'
    assign controls_class = controls_class | append: ' feature-cards__controls--mobile'
  endif

  assign has_controls = false
  if cs_is_carousel and cs_nav or cs_is_carousel and cs_pagination != 'none'
    assign has_controls = true
  endif

  comment
    ---- the gaps ----

    A Desktop/Mobile pair, published as section-private tokens and mapped onto
    `--grid-gap-x` / `--grid-gap-y` in `base.css` rather than written to those
    two names directly.

    That indirection is the whole point. A section's settings land in a `style`
    attribute, and an inline custom property beats every rule in every
    stylesheet — so a section that wrote `--grid-gap-x` itself could never be
    stepped down in a media query, at any specificity, and a Mobile slider here
    would be a control that moves nothing. Writing a private token instead lets
    the cascade in `base.css` pick which of the two applies at a given width.

    `--grid-gap-*` is still what the row is finally spaced by, so the pre-init
    grid, the `.feature-cards` grid layout and `carousel-vars`' slide widths all
    keep reading the one pair of names they already read. `carousel.js` measures
    the track's used `column-gap` and re-measures on a breakpoint change, so
    Swiper's `spaceBetween` follows the step-down without being told about it.

    `plus: 0` rather than `default` alone: 0 is a gap a merchant can legitimately
    want here — the design's panel has its cards flush against a shared frame —
    and `default` would replace it. The mobile pair takes the same treatment
    through an explicit `nil` test, because `default` cannot tell a deliberate 0
    from an unanswered setting.
  endcomment
  assign gap_x = s.gap_horizontal | default: 24 | plus: 0
  assign gap_y = s.gap_vertical | default: 24 | plus: 0

  assign gap_x_mobile = s.gap_horizontal_mobile | plus: 0
  if s.gap_horizontal_mobile == nil
    assign gap_x_mobile = 12
  endif
  assign gap_y_mobile = s.gap_vertical_mobile | plus: 0
  if s.gap_vertical_mobile == nil
    assign gap_y_mobile = 12
  endif

  assign card_stagger = s.animation_stagger | default: 120 | plus: 0
-%}

{%- capture section_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', section: s, declarations: true -%}
  --fc-gap-x: {{ gap_x }}px;
  --fc-gap-y: {{ gap_y }}px;
  --fc-gap-x-mobile: {{ gap_x_mobile }}px;
  --fc-gap-y-mobile: {{ gap_y_mobile }}px;
  --feature-cards-radius: {{ r_tl }}px {{ r_tr }}px {{ r_br }}px {{ r_bl }}px;
  --feature-cards-radius-mobile: {{ r_tl_m }}px {{ r_tr_m }}px {{ r_br_m }}px {{ r_bl_m }}px;
  --feature-cards-margin-block-start: {{ margin_top }}px;
  --feature-cards-margin-block-end: {{ margin_bottom }}px;
  {%- if border_any > 0 -%}
    --feature-cards-border-width: {{ bw_top }}px {{ bw_right }}px {{ bw_bottom }}px {{ bw_left }}px;
    --feature-cards-border-style: {{ border_line }};
    --feature-cards-border-color: {{ border_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if background_paint != '' -%}
    background: {{ background_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture panel_style -%}
  --feature-cards-panel-radius: {{ panel_radius }}px;
  --feature-cards-panel-radius-mobile: {{ panel_radius_mobile }}px;
  --feature-cards-panel-padding: {{ panel_padding }}px;
  --feature-cards-panel-padding-mobile: {{ panel_padding_mobile }}px;
  --feature-cards-controls-gap: {{ s.carousel_controls_gap | default: 24 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- if panel_width > 0 -%}
    --feature-cards-panel-border-width: {{ panel_width }}px;
    --feature-cards-panel-border-style: {{ panel_line }};
    --feature-cards-panel-border-color: {{ panel_paint }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if panel_background != '' -%}
    --feature-cards-panel-background: {{ panel_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

<div
  class="feature-cards color-scheme-container color-{{ s.color_scheme | default: 'scheme-1' }}{% if has_background_media %} has-background-media{% endif %}"
  data-section-spacing
  {%- if s.stack_layer != blank and s.stack_layer != 'default' %} data-section-layer="{{ s.stack_layer }}"{% endif %}
  data-section-id="{{ section.id }}"
  style="{{ section_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
>
  {%- if has_background_media -%}
    {% render 'background-media', settings: s %}
  {%- endif -%}

  <div class="{% render 'container', width: s.container_width %}">
    {%- comment -%}
      The merchant's heading cluster, as a static block so it cannot be dragged
      into the row of cards and cannot be deleted by accident. Same mechanism,
      same reason, as `sections/main-faq.liquid`'s `faq-header`.

      `children: true` staggers the cluster's own parts — the pill, the heading,
      the paragraph — so the section opens as one cascade rather than as a wall
      of text arriving at once.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <motion-effect
      class="feature-cards__header feature-cards__header--{{ s.alignment | default: 'center' }}"
      {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', section: s, children: true %}
    >
      {% content_for 'block', type: 'section-header', id: 'feature-cards-header' %}
    </motion-effect>

    {%- comment -%}
      One `<motion-effect>` for the whole row — see the note at the top on why
      the cards are found by selector rather than carrying an effect each.

      `kind: 'image'` rather than `'text'`: a card is a picture and a panel, and
      the text family's presets split their target into words and letters.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <motion-effect
      class="feature-cards__panel{% if panel_clipped %} feature-cards__panel--clipped{% endif %}"
      style="{{ panel_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
      {% render 'motion-attrs',
         kind: 'image',
         section: s,
         target: '.feature-card',
         stagger: card_stagger %}
    >
      {%- comment -%}
        The carousel element, written out here rather than through
        `snippets/carousel-shell.liquid`.

        Its slides are theme blocks, and `{% content_for %}` output cannot be
        captured — Shopify renders blocks with their editor bindings attached,
        and buffering that into a variable is not something the platform
        supports. So the element and its track live here while
        `carousel-shell-attrs` and `carousel-shell-tail` supply the attributes
        and the controls from the same sub-snippets the shell itself uses.
        `sections/multicolumn.liquid` does the same, for the same reason.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <swiper-carousel
        {% render 'carousel-shell-attrs',
           settings: s,
           id: carousel_id,
           layout: cs_layout,
           layout_mobile: cs_layout_mobile,
           classes: 'feature-cards__carousel',
           columns_desktop: desktop,
           columns_tablet: cols_tablet,
           columns_mobile: cols_mobile,
           count: cs_count %}
      >
        <div
          class="carousel-shell__track feature-cards__grid"
          data-ref="track"
          {% if cs_is_carousel %}aria-label="{{ cs_label | escape }}"{% endif %}
        >
          {% content_for 'blocks' %}
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          `detached` keeps the pause button and the live region — both are found
          by ref and announced from inside the element — and stands the arrows
          and the pagination down so they can be rendered below the panel
          instead. Without it the section would draw each of them twice.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {% render 'carousel-shell-tail',
           settings: s,
           layout: cs_layout,
           layout_mobile: cs_layout_mobile,
           detached: true %}
      </swiper-carousel>
    </motion-effect>

    {%- if has_controls -%}
      <div class="{{ controls_class }}">
        {%- comment -%}
          `controls` is one bar carrying its own arrows, so the bare pair would
          be a duplicate of what it already draws — the same test
          `carousel-shell-tail` makes when the controls are nested.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if cs_pagination == 'controls' -%}
          {% render 'carousel-controls',
             settings: s,
             carousel: carousel_id,
             align: cs_pagination_align %}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- if cs_nav -%}
            {% render 'carousel-nav',
               settings: s,
               carousel: carousel_id,
               position: 'bottom' %}
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- if cs_pagination != 'none' -%}
            {% render 'carousel-pagination',
               style: cs_pagination,
               align: cs_pagination_align,
               carousel: carousel_id %}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      `nil`, not `blank`. `false == blank` is true in Liquid, so a blank test
      reads "the merchant turned it off" as "never answered" and puts the footer
      back. `nil` is the only value that means the setting was never stored —
      the same guard `carousel_on_mobile` uses above.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign show_footer = s.show_footer
      if show_footer == nil
        assign show_footer = true
      endif
    -%}
    {%- if show_footer -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The line under the row, as a second static `section-header` block.

        The same mechanism as the header above and for the same reason — it
        cannot be dragged into the row of cards and cannot be deleted by
        accident — but a separate `id`, so the two clusters hold their own
        settings and a merchant editing one does not move the other.

        It is a cluster rather than a single Text block because the thing that
        follows a feature row is not always a sentence: a store often wants the
        sentence *and* the button that acts on it. The design has only the
        sentence, so that is what the preset ships; the room for the button
        costs nothing until it is used.

        Gated on a checkbox rather than on the block having content. A static
        block always renders, so with nothing in it the row would still carry
        the footer's top margin — an empty gap under the panel with no visible
        cause and no control to remove it. Turning the checkbox off keeps the
        block's settings, so switching it back on restores what was written.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <motion-effect
        class="feature-cards__footer feature-cards__footer--{{ s.footer_alignment | default: 'center' }}"
        {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', section: s, children: true %}
      >
        {% content_for 'block', type: 'section-header', id: 'feature-cards-footer' %}
      </motion-effect>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.feature_cards.name",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "_feature-card"
    },
    {
      "type": "@app"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "alignment",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.alignment.label",
      "info": "t:sections.feature_cards.settings.alignment.info",
      "options": [
